#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167736} :activity/id #uuid "516783f0-a779-5431-8277-4176b11aecbb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049374 :topic/id #uuid "efecc103-69a4-5160-9e24-2c7502edddb5" :topic/math-academy-id 3275} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168121 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is an exponential random variable with rate parameter $\\lambda > 0.$ \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)\n$$\n\n \n\nThe mean (expected value) of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}.\n$$\n\nTo prove this, first recall that the PDF of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/8c/8cdd482110e21d62a33d7d1e988d6420ab72e4b0a051ce1afea756214d217f63.svg) \n\nBy definition, \n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x f(x)\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand since $f(x) = 0$ for $x < 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\int_{0}^\\infty  \\lambda xe^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using integration by parts. First, we set\n\n$$\nu = x, \\qquad v' = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda x},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nu' = 1  \\qquad v = -e^{-\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the by-parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\lambda x{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-x{e}^{-\\lambda x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-x{e}^{-\\lambda x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }-{[\\frac{{e}^{-\\lambda x}}{\\lambda }]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6089f8ca-c6f8-50aa-ab3e-7d8c0bf61988" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5576 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "17bbd4dc-a500-572e-a9a1-71c4fa95bd18" :step/math-academy-id 34474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168122 :tutorial/content "If the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda),$ then the variance of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm {Var}[X] = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda^2}.\n$$\n\nTo prove this, we first recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm E[X]\\right)^2.\n$$\n\nAs we saw previously,\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\int_0^\\infty \\lambda xe^{-\\lambda x}\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1\\lambda,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\textrm E[X]\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda^2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\textrm E[X^2].$ First, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X^2] = \\int_0^\\infty \\lambda x^2e^{-\\lambda x}\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using integration by-parts. First, we set\n\n$$\nu = x^2, \\qquad v' =\\lambda e^{-\\lambda x},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nu' = 2x, \\qquad v = -e^{-\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the by-parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\lambda {x}^{2}{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{x}^{2}{e}^{-\\lambda x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-\\lambda x} \\\\  & =0+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-\\lambda x} \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express this integral in terms of $\\textrm E[X]$ (and therefore $\\lambda$) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-\\lambda x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\lambda }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\lambda x{e}^{-\\lambda x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\cdot \\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{\\lambda }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{\\lambda }^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "5b5a855b-86dc-5af5-8bc8-077b7cb52777" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5591 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Variance Formula"} :step/id #uuid "19d32a66-e94c-5f66-9e43-24ed356420cb" :step/math-academy-id 34494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166855 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166708 :question/id #uuid "707a9f77-49ed-5ab2-abc9-5fbffb713336" :question/math-academy-id "e-9242"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd76ab70-7c62-5513-8afa-7b805b3b8447" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435633}] :knowledge-point/title "The Mean of an Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "18ddc26f-ba45-5a89-9851-164601440d58" :step/math-academy-id 34545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166709 :question/id #uuid "5ebef25c-14db-5d26-ae2e-d4f818833795" :question/math-academy-id "e-9244"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "269d8bf1-f092-5352-a689-35b2978b163b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049786}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442841}] :knowledge-point/title "The Variance of a  Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "bdb4b74c-d4ea-59a6-bd46-301fd7e3ecf1" :step/math-academy-id 34551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166721 :question/id #uuid "2f1173e0-dd6c-5be4-a5ac-0580c6068450" :question/math-academy-id "e-9839"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddb0f6dc-ae8d-59e3-abf9-744916a0bfb1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442844}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With the Mean of an Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "f8d1c30d-1b00-59b5-942a-258b2d01bbdc" :step/math-academy-id 37012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167737}}] :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167038}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442849} :activity/id #uuid "80633357-7af9-5c1a-9cb9-41c3ac411d31" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049374 :topic/id #uuid "efecc103-69a4-5160-9e24-2c7502edddb5" :topic/math-academy-id 3275} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe74356-49b8-543c-8d6e-d896f6be8ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442821} {:answer/id #uuid "894f80a8-661c-52a9-bf68-6f0f805aa1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442827} {:answer/id #uuid "991ebd97-1bba-53d6-8eb0-37ded272c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442830} {:answer/id #uuid "ac92feca-9b24-5ba3-8997-1c3b5fdc560b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442832} {:answer/id #uuid "bba18b49-af2d-51f5-a5ee-8ea2b7824778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442832} :answer-field/id #uuid "972739f9-2ca7-5e51-8be2-cdc627574abe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ced14e8-8144-5a90-a159-85ee1ea70744" :question/math-academy-id "q-138314" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in weeks, between two grandkids visiting their grandma can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(7).$ If a grandkid has just visited their grandma, what is the average time until the next grandkid visits their grandma?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(7).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =7,$ which means that grandkids visit their grandma at an average rate of $7$ visits per week. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{week}=1\\,\\text{day}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next grandkid's visit is $1$ day."} :step/id #uuid "5cd54c92-39ad-5b9a-a09b-fe3547af99e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13aa5558-5072-53e7-bd58-cf7c7ba5a133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$15$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442819} {:answer/id #uuid "33219482-ef34-597b-b10a-a5cf5d960dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442822} {:answer/id #uuid "530f6bf8-4fcb-5c94-9cee-6007d643f0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442824} {:answer/id #uuid "8acfbc95-a05b-5d37-8140-8a62e5da7a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$30$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442828} {:answer/id #uuid "d06dab70-fbd9-57eb-9947-fb23ab39c34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442834} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7049d2d-fc9f-5f20-8e5c-02896d997034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bbd9b202-0edb-5252-b84c-96bc38834bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132627" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in minutes, between two visitors entering a restaurant can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).$ If a visitor has just entered the restaurant, what is the average time until the next visitor enters?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{min}=2\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next visitor is $2$ minutes."} :step/id #uuid "778e6bc3-396f-57e9-ad00-0591e91cecbb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fdcd7a-935f-5b89-954e-94cfe53551b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442818} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec31d2e-690a-53af-8152-9fa7d7a9b34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442823} {:answer/id #uuid "6af5ae31-0114-5767-80ce-a1faf0e59131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442826} {:answer/id #uuid "d09b5346-0b32-599f-8cfd-4d40bae72ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442835} {:answer/id #uuid "f43ab765-767d-579c-9eae-df2ab5f23f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442818} :answer-field/id #uuid "381abdbc-0501-57da-9b23-4cb39840288c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0dc4b9d8-5da7-5586-a518-10322d637373" :question/math-academy-id "q-132362" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d0c54f8f-efb1-5756-b356-6ea276ef3ad3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05547d8f-b729-55a9-8be3-036d66839167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442817} {:answer/id #uuid "2520da57-e0e2-58df-b3cf-ec9420890983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442820} {:answer/id #uuid "611a9703-d8be-5d7c-b587-568f34b6ac1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442825} {:answer/id #uuid "902763c5-4b29-5719-bd61-94b503ffbf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442829} {:answer/id #uuid "9f233499-e299-5d78-8480-e3546726af7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442831} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efe295c-e280-5ef7-a7b5-b62fe5c12fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "358a711e-17d9-56a3-a7f2-d4538d452099" :question/math-academy-id "q-138321" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in days, between two doses of medication in a patient's treatment can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(6).$ If a dose has just been taken by the patient, what is the average time until the next dose?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(6).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =6,$ which means that $6$ doses are taken each day. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{day}=4\\,\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next dose is $4$ hours."} :step/id #uuid "d1a1ba80-2f4c-549a-969a-7ffdffc5fabd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1608335-135d-518f-b0f8-9d14f1148e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163547} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4d0544-6ad8-5103-8c56-091eafd7f5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163548} {:answer/id #uuid "049f0e31-087f-545b-a089-f313f3063bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163549} {:answer/id #uuid "0e250c9e-c165-578b-9fe5-a70487768653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163550} {:answer/id #uuid "23e3423b-695d-56f2-9c74-65be2374abc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163550} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b75e96e-6286-569e-8a97-9f97357e7c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b606a84e-9f53-5e58-b319-b9877d0150ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-112759" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.2),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.2),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(0.2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0.04} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec507f4e-7ad4-574b-b220-b028ea07092f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442847}}] :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442816}]}
